<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Man of 83 with stage IIA, hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative right breast cancer, fourteen weeks after bilateral mastectomy and right axillary dissection, on tamoxifen for eleven weeks, started at another hospital. Please take over his adjuvant endocrine therapy, confirm the adjuvant plan, and arrange germline genetic testing. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is tamoxifen the right adjuvant endocrine therapy for him, and for how long?</item><item>Is any other adjuvant treatment, such as chemotherapy or radiation, indicated now?</item><item>Which germline test should he have, and what would a result mean for him and his relatives?</item><item>How should tamoxifen safety be monitored at his age, and which medicines should he avoid?</item><item>What surveillance does he need after bilateral mastectomy, and what should be done about his bone health?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Brooke is seen at the request of Dr. Bellamy to take over his breast cancer care. He noticed discomfort in his right breast, which he dismissed for a time, before imaging and biopsy showed cancer; he says he did not know men could get breast cancer. He had a bilateral mastectomy and right axillary dissection at another hospital about four months ago, and started tamoxifen four weeks later. He has taken it daily for fifteen weeks with mild night hot flashes and no other effects. He has no bone pain, cough, headache, or weight loss. He is independent, walks most days, and has no history of blood clots, stroke, or liver disease. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Edward William Brooke III was born in Washington, D.C., on October 26, 1919. [D]</item><item>Brooke was elected attorney general of Massachusetts in 1962 and re-elected in 1964, and in 1966 became the first African American elected to the U.S. Senate by popular vote; he served as a Republican senator from Massachusetts from January 3, 1967, to January 3, 1979. [D]</item><item>In a CNN interview aired on June 11, 2003, Brooke said that in September 2002 he felt discomfort in his right breast, which he dismissed as aging, possible muscle strain, or gas; that he then had a mammogram, a sonogram, and a needle biopsy, which found a malignant tumor; that his breasts had never before been examined by a doctor; and that he had not thought men got breast cancer. [D]</item><item>In the same interview Brooke said he had to be operated on for breast cancer, that the cancer had spread to his lymph nodes, and that all of the lymph nodes on his right side were removed. [D]</item><item>An Associated Press report of June 11, 2003, said that Brooke, 83, had been diagnosed with breast cancer in September, had a double mastectomy, and was free of the cancer, and that he was encouraging men to examine themselves and urging insurers to cover mammograms for men. [D]</item><item>In the 2003 interview Brooke described himself as not a smoker or a drinker and said he exercised and ate fairly good food. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

